I've yet to see dragon scale mail in a shop.
I'd probably get pretty excited if I did see
some, granted, and I'd definitely want to
purchase it.  But so far nobody stocks it.

I've seen mithril in a shop, but far more often
I've picked mithril off a corpse.  Ditto for any
kind of cloak you care to name.

I do fairly frequently get boots or gloves
from a shop, but they're not expensive.

Shirts usually come from bones, but that's
okay, because they're of no practical value
until you have a surplus of enchant weapon
scrolls, by which time you can typically
uncurse things as well.

> I do similar. It depends on my status, alignment, and first of
> all, whether the mines are lit or not. Having a lamp may adjust
> priorities. So it depends.

It does depend.  Lamps run out, but the number of dark
levels in the mines varies enormously.  As best I can tell,
there can be anywhere from 0 to 3 of them before you
get to Minetown, and anywhere from 0 to 12 before you
get to Mine's End.  Being in the dark is dangerous in
any case, but without a lamp (or equivalent) it's even
more dangerous.

> > I've been placing high priority on getting some armor
> > and a weapon as soon as possible.  Is that a mistake?
>
> No, that's fine. Mind, though, that plate mail is nothing for
> weak characters.

I generally only wear plate mail if I haven't found
any other armor for that slot.  (This happens
sometimes to a very early Tourist:  given the
option of a Hawaiian shirt and plate mail or just
a Hawaiian shirt, I generally drop other things
and wear the mail until I can find something
lighter.)

> Finding mithril may be a intermediate goal,

I make a practice of pet-testing every mithril coat
I see until I'm wearing one (unless I'm a Monk or
get extremely lucky and find DSM first).  But
mithril is only light when you compare it to heavy
things like plate mail.

> > Should I be leaving that mail and cloak laying on the
> > floor and running around in just a Hawaiian shirt?
>
> No. Definitely not. You need the (non-apparent)
> MC factor of the cloak, and you should get your
> AC down as much and as early as possible.

Okay, that confirms what I thought I knew.

> > Should I be leaving the magic items (scrolls and
> > potions and wands) all laying on the ground?
>
> If unidentified, yes, definitely.

Ah.

At some point I'll have to drag them to a shop
for price-checking, but I suppose that can wait
until I find a shop.

> But keep those in some safe place.

> Wands, though, can typically be easily idientified by
> "engrave-testing".

And the ones that don't do anything when you
engrave and also don't do anything when you
zap them at yourself aren't worth having.  Yeah,
I've learned how to identify wands.

Scrolls are more problematic (i.e., I haven't
really found a good way to ID most of them
yet), and potions are a bit riskier -- on more
than one occasion early quaff-testing has
killed me despite engraving Elbereth before
doing it.

> And Sokoban is a good source of
> wands; one reason to do it early (i.e. before the mines).

Sokoban is also a good source of food, which
tends to be more of a problem in the early game
than at any other time (except maybe the very
late game; I've yet to obtain the AoY, so I have
no experience with the final run).

Also, Sokoban comes right about the time
that the main dungeon starts wanting to
generate really dangerous monsters, like
rope golems and owlbears and whatnot.
Spending some time in Sokoban pushing
boulders around gives you a chance to
boost your stats a bit before you have
to face them.

Also, there's a 50% chance of getting
a bag of holding, which is high on my
list of the most desirable objects in the
game.  Also high on this list is a
spellbook of identify (unless you're a
non-spellcasting role like Barbarian)
and dragon scale mail.


